          "question": "I get short of breath climbing two flights of stairs but I am completely fine at rest.",
          "answer": "**Urgency: routine.** The red-flag check did not match an emergency pattern. That is not a clearance. The check covers a fixed list of presentations, so a symptom it does not list can still be serious. Because this symptom is new and not yet explained, it is still worth arranging a routine appointment and mentioning it to a clinician.\n\n**Seek urgent or emergency care if any of these develop:**\n- Breathlessness at rest, or difficulty speaking in full sentences (COPD and asthma passages).\n- Chest pain, coughing up blood, blue lips or fingers, confusion, or unusual drowsiness (COPD and asthma passages).\n- Breathlessness that gets worse, or does not respond to usual treatment (COPD passage).\n\nThe retrieved passages did not cover palpitations, fainting, or new leg or ankle swelling, so I cannot cite guidance on them. Treat any of these alongside breathlessness as a reason to contact a clinician promptly, and call emergency services if they are severe.\n\n**Possible causes to ask a clinician about, grouped by body system:**\n- **Breathing and lungs:** chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD), asthma, and respiratory infections such as the common cold or influenza. These are in the retrieved guidance, but the analysis did not confirm that any of them fits this pattern.\n- **Heart and circulation:** the retrieved passages did not cover this system, so I cannot name causes from them. A clinician would normally consider it for exertional breathlessness, and it is worth raising specifically.\n- **Muscles and joints:** osteoarthritis is in the retrieved guidance, but its passage does not link it to breathlessness, so it is unlikely to explain this on its own.\n\nThis is not a diagnosis. Only a clinician who examines you can say what is causing it. Note when it started, how far you can go before it starts, and whether it is getting worse, and bring that to your appointment.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
